+(duration-type)=
+
+### Duration
+
+The {class}`Duration` type converts a length of time into a
+{class}`datetime.timedelta`. The value is either a whole number of seconds,
+like `120`, or one or more `<integer><unit>` groups using the units `d`
+(days), `h` (hours), `m` (minutes), and `s` (seconds), like `2d`, `90m`,
+or `1h30m`. Each unit may appear at most once and in that order, with no spaces
+between groups. Units are case-insensitive, so `45S` is the same as `45s`.
+Negative numbers and decimals are not accepted. A `timedelta` default is used
+as-is.
+
+```{eval-rst}
+.. click:example::
+
+    @click.command()
+    @click.option("--timeout", type=click.Duration(), default="30s")
+    def wait(timeout):
+        click.echo(f"Waiting {timeout.total_seconds():g} seconds")
+
+.. click:run::
+
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1h30m'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=90'])
+    invoke(wait, args=['--timeout=1.5h'])
+```
+
